A||N||7||Don||Gear change on 4T65E transaxle transplant||||1||
Z||000000||Don||12-11-2000||04:47 PM||||I installed a 3800 supercharged Buick V-6 with the companion 4T65E transaxle from a '97 Grand Prix to a 1988 Fiero. In the transition I changed the gear ratio from 2:93 to 3:68 gearing it down. At normal driving the transaxle shifts up fine but at wide open throttle the RPMs hit the rev limiter before it shifts.<P>I need help as to what to do to make the transaxle shift at 5800 RPMs at wide open throttle and the speedometer work properly. I understand the computer measures the input shaft speed and the output shaft speed and they have to be in a certain range to make the transaxle shift properly. I've tried a couple different people to work this out by reprogramming the comptuer but no one has been able to do it to date.  HELP!||||reg||1
Z||000001||rockcrawl||12-11-2000||05:03 PM||||On the older non-electronic versions, it was as simple as changing the governor that mounts in the tail end where the VSS is, but I don't know if the E-trans uses a mechanical governor. Where did you get the gearset? Just curious.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Dan86gt3.8||12-11-2000||05:57 PM||||This sounds like a question for terry k.  Also I would be real interested in where you got your gear sets.  Also what do you turn rpm wise at 70?<P>------------------<BR>86 gt fiero<BR>3.8/4speed auto<BR>ace conversion kit<BR>1/4- 15.3@90mph||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||12-11-2000||10:50 PM||||Your N/V ratio is hosed now. This is the RPM/MPH. It's fixed for each gear. This is how the PCM can tell what gear it's in and determine if there is any trans slippage, turn on the shift light (your's doesn't have that), and some other stuff.<P>When it's wrong, several things happen. One you've discovered. You need to find the donor car for your final drive, get the VIN, and then get the PCM reflashed with that code. I'm sure some dealer will do it. Just tell them you want the latest update for that VIN. I hope you used a final drive from an existing car and didn't mix gears (where no N/V ratio combination exists).<P>As for the speedo, it will be wrong depending on your tire size. The road speed constant and divisor is set for 225/60-16's. With the different final drive, it's going to be all wonky. A DRAC can solve that. You can drive the speedo from the PCM by using a 2.7k pullup resistor to +12V and then feeding the signal through a 10uF cap to the speedo. Try each wire to the speedo. One will work.<P>Another possible solution is to use the DRAC to change the VSS *output* to be what the ECM expects. Of course your speedo will be hosed again. You can rescale the speedo with a pot, but the odo and trip will be wrong. To make them both right, you need a variable oscillator circuit to replace the crystal. Then you just dial up the right speedo reading and the odo and trip will be correct too.<P>DRAC = Digital Ratio Adapter. Dakota Digital and some others sell them. They call them something different.<P>So, you have a choice. Reflash the PCM with the correct code for the final drive (if it exists), try to fake the PCM out by altering the VSS output signal, drop the 4T65E and switch to a 4T60E and run 94/95 PCM and code. The speedo reading right can be dealt with seperately.<P>You can contact Thrasher (www.thrasher-ep.com?) and see if they will do it. You will need a scanner to read the RPM and MPH on each gear, divide and then supply that to them. They can then flash that back into the PCM. I know they can do it, it just a question of *will they*.<P>If someone knows another solution, I'd like to hear it.<P>Terry<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Dan86gt3.8||12-12-2000||03:15 AM||||Well I just called NASA and they said to call you.  The only other thing I can think of is find a 86-88 DMC time machine, that still has a good flux capacitor and that should fix everything.  Man you got to love terry!  He knows everything you ever wanted to know about fiero electronics!<BR>seriously though I had no idea on how to fix it, I just knew it sounded like a job for terry!<P>------------------<BR>86 gt fiero<BR>3.8/4speed auto<BR>ace conversion kit<BR>1/4- 15.3@90mph||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||12-12-2000||12:02 PM||||Never have I been complemented in a such a negative tone!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Sorry, I've just spent a lot of time on it. Too much time. Ask the wife. Ask the dog.<P>I'll condense it....<P>The PCM needs to know the gear ratio's which include the final drive. It they are wrong, it barfs. When it barfs, it don't work good. Make right, it works gooder. Leave wrong and it bad juju.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I'm more than happy to clarify. <BR><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Don||12-13-2000||08:51 PM||||Thanks for helping out with your suggestions.  As you guessed, I did mix gears where no car of I know of uses this gear ratio combination. The closest to the 3:68 ratio I have is the 3:29 in a Z34 Monte Carlo. If there is one out there, it has to be in a Pontiac as I found out from Thrasher. The computer program is not compatible from one division to another. Thrasher actually reprogrammed my computer to a Regal that they had modified with the same gear ratio but the car would not run on that reprogram. It would just start and shut off right away - we tried a couple of times to reprogram by taking out the security code but to no avail. I will contact them again on your suggestions and hopefully they can help. I have another thought, would going to 3:29 gears work with my Pontiac system or is the computer going to catch me on that gear ratio too?<P>I found a '97 computer is also different from a '98 and up. <BR>Thanks again for any thoughts you might have.||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||12-13-2000||11:45 PM||||Hey, I don't guess. I know. When I don't know, I fake it! Kidding....<P>Well, I know they (Thrasher) has the code for the PCM. If you measure the RPM and MPH in each gear, they should be able to go in and change the N/V ratio's for the 4 gears. The 92-95's only did 3rd and 4th. I think the 97 and up did all of them. If Thrasher does this, then you should be able to use the gears you have. Close ain't close enough.<P>The PCM will choke if the RPM/MPH is beyond about +/- 2 (that's a common range). It just gets back to what your PCM is set for. Put another way, there are no gear ratio's that will work on the same N/V numbers that I know of. The 2.97/3.08's might be, but I think the gears themselves throw it outside the window.<P>Look at this:<P>3RD LOWER LIMIT 41<BR>3RD UPPER LIMIT 37<BR>4TH LOWER LIMIT 29<BR>4TH UPPER LIMIT 25<BR> <BR>This are the N/V ratio's limits for my 3800 SC Fiero. 3rd should be about 39 and 4th about 27. <P>At 2500 RPM in 3rd, the MPH is 64 MPH, so 2500 RPM/64 MPH is 39. So the lower limit is 41 and the upper limit is 37. You don't get much room. Over the whole RPM range for 3rd gear, that ratio will never change (unless something is slipping). <P>If it goes outside the limits, the ECM throws a code and disables TCC. If it happens in 4th gear, it throws a code, diables 4th (drops to third) and disables TCC. This is a clever way of checking trans slip and saving it.  By the way, this is all checked when the TCC is locked. The PCM ignores these limits until the TCC locks (TCC slip will be under 25 RPM or so), then it checks.<P>If you change the gear ratios, the N/V number changes and it only takes a change of 4 MPH to throw it outside the limits. <P>For me to calc the N/V ratio you need, I'd need the 1-4 gear ratios and the final drive ratio. The problem is, I don't know what the PCM is programmed at. <P>I wonder if the 4T65E shift performance solendoids can be hard-wired. Then you could switch to a 92/93 or 94/95 ECM and all of this pesky N/V stuff can be fixed much easier.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
